relocobj_* -- PowerPC and MIPS relocatable objects whose calls and addresses
are relocations (GH-904; tests/stages/kuna-relocobj-ppc-mips.xml).

What they are
  relocobj_ppc32_O2.o      PowerPC32 BE, -fno-pic: REL24 calls, ADDR16_HA/LO
                           addresses, REL32 in .eh_frame.
  relocobj_ppc32_pic_O2.o  PowerPC32 BE, -fPIC: PLTREL24 calls (addend 0x8000,
                           the .got2 offset), .got2 ADDR32 words, REL32.
  relocobj_ppc64_O2.o      PowerPC64 BE ELFv1: function symbols in .opd,
                           REL24 calls against them, TOC16 halves, R_PPC64_TOC.
  relocobj_mips32_O2.o     MIPS32 BE o32, -fno-pic -mno-abicalls: R_MIPS_26
                           calls, REL HI16/LO16 address pairs.
  relocobj_mipsel_pic_O2.o MIPS32 LE o32, PIC (the default): _gp_disp
                           prologues, CALL16 calls with R_MIPS_JALR hints,
                           GOT16/LO16 addresses.
  Source: relocobj_calls.c.

Why they are vendored
  CI has no PowerPC or MIPS toolchain, and the assertions depend on the exact
  instructions and relocations.

Where they came from
  Ubuntu clang 14.0.0 (host) and Debian mips-linux-gnu-gcc 12.2.0-14 (docker):

      clang -target powerpc-linux-gnu -O2 -fno-pic -c relocobj_calls.c -o relocobj_ppc32_O2.o
      clang -target powerpc-linux-gnu -O2 -fPIC -c relocobj_calls.c -o relocobj_ppc32_pic_O2.o
      clang -target powerpc64-linux-gnu -O2 -c relocobj_calls.c -o relocobj_ppc64_O2.o
      clang -target mipsel-linux-gnu -O2 -c relocobj_calls.c -o relocobj_mipsel_pic_O2.o
      mips-linux-gnu-gcc -O2 -fno-pic -mno-abicalls -c relocobj_calls.c -o relocobj_mips32_O2.o

